package settings

import (
	"net/url"
	"time"
)

// DefaultMissingTransactionsFetchTimeout is the default for
// subtreevalidation_missingTransactionsFetchTimeout, and the value a non-positive
// setting falls back to.
const DefaultMissingTransactionsFetchTimeout = 10 * time.Minute

// SubtreeValidationSettings configures the Merkle subtree validation service.
type SubtreeValidationSettings struct {
	QuorumPath                                string              `key:"subtree_quorum_path" desc:"Path for quorum configuration" default:"" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Location of quorum configuration file" type:"string" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSpecifies the file path to quorum configuration for subtree validation consensus.\n\n### How It Works\nQuorum configuration defines which validators must agree for a subtree to be considered valid. The configuration file specifies validator endpoints and quorum thresholds for Byzantine fault tolerance.\n\n### Recommendations\n- **Multi-validator deployments** - Provide path to quorum configuration for fault tolerance\n- **Single-validator setups** - Leave empty (no quorum needed)"`
	QuorumAbsoluteTimeout                     time.Duration       `key:"subtree_quorum_absolute_timeout" desc:"Absolute timeout for subtree quorum" default:"30s" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Maximum wait for quorum agreement" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSets the maximum time to wait for quorum agreement on subtree validation.\n\n### How It Works\nWhen multiple validators participate in subtree validation, they must reach agreement within this timeout. If quorum is not reached, validation fails and the subtree is rejected.\n\n### Trade-offs\n| Setting | Benefit | Drawback |\n|---------|---------|----------|\n| Shorter | Faster failure detection | May timeout on slow networks |\n| Longer | More tolerant of latency | Slower failure recovery |\n\n### Recommendations\n- **30s** (default) - Reasonable for most distributed deployments\n- Increase for high-latency or geographically distributed validators"`
	SubtreeStore                              *url.URL            `key:"subtreestore" desc:"URL for subtree storage backend" default:"" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Where validated subtrees are stored" type:"url" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSpecifies the storage backend URL for validated subtree data.\n\n### Format\n- **file://** - Local filesystem storage\n- **s3://** - Amazon S3 or compatible cloud storage\n\n### How It Works\nAfter successful validation, subtrees are stored at this location. The Asset Server retrieves subtrees from this store to serve syncing peers.\n\n### Recommendations\n- Use **file://** for local development or single-node deployments\n- Use **s3://** for production multi-node deployments with shared storage"`
	GetMissingTransactions                    int                 `key:"subtreevalidation_getMissingTransactions" desc:"Concurrency for fetching missing transactions" default:"auto" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Parallel missing transaction fetches (auto = NumCPU/2)" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nControls the number of concurrent workers fetching missing transactions during subtree validation.\n\n### How It Works\nWhen a subtree references transactions not in the local cache, multiple workers fetch them from peers in parallel batches. Each worker handles a batch of transaction requests.\n\n### Values\n- **auto** (default) - Uses NumCPU/2 for balanced performance\n- **N > 0** - Fixed number of workers\n\n### Trade-offs\n| Setting | Benefit | Drawback |\n|---------|---------|----------|\n| Higher | Faster transaction retrieval | Higher network and peer load |\n| Lower | Reduced resource usage | Slower subtree validation |\n\n### Recommendations\n- Use **auto** for most deployments\n- Set explicitly for resource-constrained environments"`
	GRPCAddress                               string              `key:"subtreevalidation_grpcAddress" desc:"gRPC address for Subtree Validation service" default:"localhost:8089" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Address for inter-service communication" type:"string" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSpecifies the client connection address for the Subtree Validation gRPC service.\n\n### Format\n**host:port** - Where host is a hostname, IP address, or DNS name.\n\n### Examples\n- **localhost:8089** - Local development (default)\n- **subtreevalidation.internal:8089** - Internal DNS\n- **10.0.1.51:8089** - Direct IP address\n\n### Recommendations\n- Use internal DNS or service mesh addresses in production\n- Use localhost only for local development"`
	GRPCListenAddress                         string              `key:"subtreevalidation_grpcListenAddress" desc:"gRPC listen address for Subtree Validation service" default:":8089" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Port for incoming gRPC connections" type:"string" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSpecifies the server binding address for the Subtree Validation gRPC service.\n\n### Format\n- **:port** - Binds to all network interfaces\n- **ip:port** - Binds to a specific interface\n\n### Examples\n- **:8089** - Listen on all interfaces (default)\n- **127.0.0.1:8089** - Listen only on localhost\n- **10.0.1.51:8089** - Listen on specific interface\n\n### Recommendations\n- Use **:port** format for containerized deployments\n- Bind to specific IP for security in multi-tenant environments"`
	ProcessTxMetaUsingCacheConcurrency        int                 `key:"subtreevalidation_processTxMetaUsingCache_Concurrency" desc:"Cache-probe workers shared by all subtrees being validated (one pool for peer subtrees, one for block validation)" default:"32" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Server-wide parallel cache lookups" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nLimits the number of goroutines probing the txmeta cache across ALL subtrees validated at once (not per subtree). Peer-announced subtrees share one pool of this size and block validation has its own, so a burst of peer subtrees cannot delay a block.\n\n### How It Works\nEach subtree's lookups are split by cache bucket range (adjacent small ranges merged), or into contiguous spans of 4096 on the lockfree cache, which has no bucket locks; each task is probed by one worker with a single batched read. Workers come from one server-wide pool of this size, so a burst of hundreds of subtrees shares it instead of each subtree starting its own. Probe goroutines compete with the Kafka txmeta handler for the cache's bucket locks and the CPU, so this bounds how much a read flood can slow ingestion.\n\n### Recommendations\n- **32** (default) - ~50M lookups/s on a 192-core node while keeping Kafka ingestion within ~30% of an idle cache\n- Raising it increases probe throughput at the cost of ingestion latency under bursts (192 workers: ~150M lookups/s, ingestion ~12x slower)"`
	PeerSubtreeConcurrency                    int                 `key:"subtreevalidation_peerSubtreeConcurrency" desc:"Peer-announced subtrees validated at once" default:"32" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Server-wide; 0 or less disables the limit" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nBounds how many peer-announced subtrees are being validated at the same time, and so how many hold a per-subtree working set (the tx hashes, the subtree, its meta and the txmeta slice: about 1 GiB for 1M transactions).\n\n### How It Works\nA peer subtree takes a slot before it takes the subtree's quorum lock and keeps it until its validation ends; once it has one it re-checks that the node is still RUNNING, including the download, fail-fast retries and any missing-transaction fetches. A subtree waiting for a slot holds no lock, so block validation never waits behind the queue; if the subtree is validated meanwhile (by block validation), it is skipped once it gets a slot. Subtrees validated for a block do not use this limit. Without it, the backlog of peer subtrees that arrives after a block (up to NumCPU at once) each allocated its working set and then queued for cache-probe workers, which pushed the Go heap past 150 GiB for a minute on the scaling cluster.\n\n### Recommendations\n- **32** (default) - on the scaling cluster a subtree takes about 1 s and steady load keeps 2-3 in flight; 32 drains a post-block burst of 10 subtrees/s while bounding the heap\n- Raise it if peer subtrees queue for long while CPU is idle (slow peers or many retries hold slots): watch teranode_subtreevalidation_peer_subtree_admission_wait and _waiting\n- **0** - no limit (the previous behaviour)"`
	SkippedSubtreeBackfillMax                 int                 `key:"subtreevalidation_skippedSubtreeBackfillMax" desc:"Peer subtrees announced during a catchup that are validated once the node is RUNNING again" default:"4096" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"0 or less turns the backfill off" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nWhile the node is catching up (CATCHINGBLOCKS) the subtree validator does not validate peer-announced subtrees. Up to this many of them are kept and validated once the node is RUNNING again, so the next block does not arrive with those subtrees unvalidated. Subtrees announced while IDLE are not kept: an operator STOP can last days, and the peers may have pruned them by then.\n\n### How It Works\nEach peer subtree announced while catching up is remembered (hash, peer URL, peer ID, deduplicated by hash, in announcement order; when full, the oldest is dropped). Reaching RUNNING starts a replay of the kept ones, oldest first, through the normal peer-subtree path, with the same admission limit, quorum lock and RUNNING check; the replay uses at most half of subtreevalidation_peerSubtreeConcurrency, and subtrees validated meanwhile (for example by a block) are skipped by the quorum lock. Entries older than 30 minutes are not replayed.\n\n### Recommendations\n- **4096** (default) - about 30 minutes of announcements at the scaling-test rate of ~2 subtrees/s\n- **0** - keep nothing (the previous behaviour)"`
	ProcessTxMetaUsingCacheMissingTxThreshold int                 `key:"subtreevalidation_processTxMetaUsingCache_MissingTxThreshold" desc:"Threshold for missing transactions when using cache" default:"1" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Switch to store after this many cache misses" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDefines when to switch from cache-based to store-based processing based on cache misses.\n\n### How It Works\nWhen processing transaction metadata, if cache misses exceed this threshold, the system falls back to store-based processing.\n\n### Values\n- **1** (default) - Fall back on any cache miss (strict)\n- **N > 1** - Tolerate N cache misses before switching\n\n### Trade-offs\n| Setting | Benefit | Drawback |\n|---------|---------|----------|\n| Lower | Consistent performance | More store fallbacks |\n| Higher | Maximizes cache use | Potential for slow mixed-mode processing |"`
	SubtreeDAHConcurrency                     int                 `key:"subtreevalidation_subtreeDAHConcurrency" desc:"Concurrency for subtree DAH operations" default:"8" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Parallel DAH operations for subtrees" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nControls the number of parallel workers for Delete-At-Height (DAH) operations on subtrees.\n\n### How It Works\nDAH marks subtrees for eventual pruning at a specified block height. Multiple workers can update DAH markers concurrently to improve throughput.\n\n### Trade-offs\n| Setting | Benefit | Drawback |\n|---------|---------|----------|\n| Higher | Faster DAH updates | More storage I/O |\n| Lower | Reduced storage load | Slower DAH processing |\n\n### Recommendations\n- **8** (default) - Good balance for most storage systems\n- Increase for high-IOPS storage systems"`
	TxMetaCacheEnabled                        bool                `key:"subtreevalidation_txMetaCacheEnabled" desc:"Enable transaction metadata caching" default:"true" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Improves validation performance" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nEnables in-memory caching of transaction metadata for faster validation.\n\n### How It Works\n- Cache is populated from the Kafka txmeta topic\n- Cache hits avoid expensive database lookups to the UTXO store\n- Essential for achieving high-throughput validation\n\n### Values\n- **true** (default) - Enable caching for production performance\n- **false** - Disable caching (debugging or extreme memory constraints only)\n\n### Recommendations\n- Keep enabled for all production deployments\n- Only disable for debugging cache-related issues"`
	TxMetaCacheMaxMB                          int                 `key:"txMetaCacheMaxMB" desc:"Maximum memory for transaction metadata cache" default:"256" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Increase for better validation performance" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSets the maximum memory in megabytes for the transaction metadata cache.\n\n### How It Works\nLarger cache size improves hit rate by retaining more transaction metadata, reducing database lookups during subtree validation.\n\n### Trade-offs\n| Setting | Benefit | Drawback |\n|---------|---------|----------|\n| Higher | Better cache hit rate | More memory usage |\n| Lower | Less memory usage | More database queries |\n\n### Recommendations\n- **256** (default) - Suitable for most deployments\n- Increase for high-throughput nodes with available memory\n- Monitor cache hit rate metrics to optimize"`
	TxMetaCacheBucketType                     string              `key:"subtreevalidation_txMetaCacheBucketType" desc:"Allocation strategy for the txmeta cache buckets" default:"unallocated" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"unallocated|preallocated|trimmed|native|pointer|lockfree" type:"string" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSelects the per-bucket allocation strategy backing the in-memory txmeta cache (stores/txmetacache.BucketType).\n\n### Values\n- **unallocated** (default) - Plain Go map per bucket, chunks allocated on demand. Lowest startup memory; current production behaviour.\n- **preallocated** - Plain Go map per bucket but chunk memory is reserved up front. Pairs with subtreevalidation_txMetaCacheTrimRatio for the trim-on-rotate path.\n- **trimmed** - Swiss-style split lock-free map per bucket plus an on-demand chunk allocator with periodic trim.\n- **native** - Native lock-free uint64 map per bucket. Smallest per-entry overhead; benefits from the inner-shard cleanup applied in the same release that introduced this setting. No long-running production telemetry yet — canary first.\n- **pointer** - Experimental pointer-backed cache: stores *meta.Data directly in sharded native maps with per-bucket FIFO key-ring eviction. Reads return the stored pointer with no copy or deserialisation. Bench shows ~4.5x faster Set, ~11.5x faster Get, and zero GC pressure on the hot loop. Trade-off: larger steady-state heap (pointers + their referents) and a slightly looser eviction guarantee (FIFO ring, not generational chunk rotation). Canary one pod first.\n- **lockfree** - Off-heap per-writer rings plus a set-associative index, with no locks on reads or writes and no index rebuild when a ring wraps (stores/txmetacache/lockfree_lanes.go). Off-heap like unallocated, so it adds no GC pressure. It asks for transparent huge pages, which take effect only where the node runs THP in madvise or always mode. Canary one pod first.\n\n### How It Works\nThe value is parsed in services/subtreevalidation/Server.go and mapped to the BucketType enum passed to txmetacache.NewTxMetaCache. Unknown values fall back to unallocated (with a warning log) so a typo never silently changes the deployed allocator.\n\n### Recommendations\n- Keep **unallocated** for any node where you do not actively monitor txmeta cache memory.\n- Try **native** on a single canary pod with production-scale TxMetaCacheMaxMB before rolling out widely. Watch RSS, cleanLockedMap allocation rate, and the subtreevalidation_set_tx_meta_cache_kafka_batch histogram (or _count counter for per-entry rate).\n- Try **pointer** on a separate canary if GC pause time on the receiver is a concern; the heap is larger but allocation churn drops to near-zero.\n- Try **lockfree** on a single canary subtree node with production-scale TxMetaCacheMaxMB, preferably with THP in madvise mode (check AnonHugePages in /proc/<pid>/smaps_rollup). Watch the Kafka txmeta consumer lag and teranode_tx_meta_cache_hits/misses against an unallocated node, and teranode_tx_meta_cache_lane_waits, which should stay at 0.\n\nThe batched cache read in processTxMetaUsingCache (GetMetaCachedInto, decoding into caller-owned storage) is used with every bucket type, not only lockfree."`
	TxMetaCacheTrimRatio                      int                 `key:"subtreevalidation_txMetaCacheTrimRatio" desc:"Trim ratio for preallocated bucket type" default:"2" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Higher = more aggressive trimming" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nControls how aggressively the preallocated bucket type trims its chunk arena when generations roll over. Only effective when TxMetaCacheBucketType=preallocated.\n\n### How It Works\nPasses through txmetacache.NewTxMetaCache to txmetacache.New and on to bucketPreallocated.Init as trimRatio. Other bucket types ignore this value.\n\n### Recommendations\n- **2** (default) - Existing behaviour for the preallocated path.\n- Increase to free chunk memory sooner under tight RAM."`
	TxChanBufferSize                          int                 `key:"subtreevalidation_txChanBufferSize" desc:"Buffer size for transaction channel" default:"0" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Channel buffer for transaction processing" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSets the buffer size for internal transaction processing channels.\n\n### How It Works\nControls the Go channel buffer size used for passing transactions between processing stages.\n\n### Values\n- **0** (default) - Unbuffered channels for synchronous processing\n- **N > 0** - Buffered channels for pipelined processing\n\n### Recommendations\n- **0** - Default for most deployments (simpler flow control)\n- Increase for pipelining when producer and consumer have variable processing times"`
	BatchMissingTransactions                  bool                `key:"subtreevalidation_batch_missing_transactions" desc:"Batch missing transaction fetches" default:"true" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Enable to batch missing transaction requests" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nEnables batching of missing transaction fetches instead of individual queries.\n\n### How It Works\n- When enabled, missing transactions are collected and fetched in batches\n- When disabled, each missing transaction is fetched individually\n\n### Trade-offs\n| Setting | Benefit | Drawback |\n|---------|---------|----------|\n| Enabled | Reduced database overhead | Latency for batch collection |\n| Disabled | Lower per-request latency | More database queries |\n\n### Recommendations\n- **true** (default) - Better performance for most deployments\n- Disable only for debugging or specific latency requirements"`
	SpendBatcherSize                          int                 `key:"subtreevalidation_spendBatcherSize" desc:"Batch size for spend operations" default:"1024" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Number of spends per batch" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nControls how many UTXO spend operations are batched together during subtree processing.\n\n### How It Works\nWhen validating transactions, UTXO spends are collected and sent to the UTXO store in batches. This also controls the concurrency limit for parallel transaction processing (SpendBatcherSize * 2).\n\n### Trade-offs\n| Setting | Benefit | Drawback |\n|---------|---------|----------|\n| Larger | Fewer database round-trips | Higher memory per batch |\n| Smaller | Lower memory usage | More database overhead |\n\n### Recommendations\n- **1024** (default) - Good balance for Aerospike performance\n- Adjust based on UTXO store characteristics"`
	MissingTransactionsBatchSize              int                 `key:"subtreevalidation_missingTransactionsBatchSize" desc:"Batch size for fetching missing transactions" default:"16384" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Number of transactions per batch fetch" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nControls the maximum number of missing transactions fetched in a single batch operation from peers.\n\n### How It Works\nWhen transactions are missing during subtree validation, they are requested from peers in batches of this size to balance network efficiency with memory usage.\n\n### Trade-offs\n| Setting | Benefit | Drawback |\n|---------|---------|----------|\n| Larger | Fewer HTTP round-trips | Higher memory per batch |\n| Smaller | Lower memory usage | More network requests |\n\n### Recommendations\n- **16384** (default) - Optimized for typical subtree sizes\n- Reduce if memory constrained\n- Increase for high-latency peer connections\n\n### Peer-auth interaction\nA POST /subtree/:hash/txs catchup request carries 32 bytes per requested tx hash, up to this batch size. Asset's peer-auth middleware caps signed request bodies at max(1MiB, 32 * the receiving node's own value of this setting + 4096) — see asset_peerAuthAllowlist. Raising this above what a peer you fetch from is running can get your requests 413'd there; raising it on a node that allowlists peers with a larger value has the same effect on requests from them. Keep this in sync across nodes that allowlist each other."`
	MissingTransactionsFetchTimeout           time.Duration       `key:"subtreevalidation_missingTransactionsFetchTimeout" desc:"Bound on one missing-transactions batch fetch" default:"10m" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Maximum time for one missing-transactions batch fetch, including retries" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nBounds one getMissingTransactionsBatch call to a peer, covering the HTTP POST, its retry ladder and the response parse.\n\n### How It Works\nWithout a deadline on this context, the retry helper's ctx.Done() abort can never fire, so the retry loop always runs to its configured attempt limit and every attempt installs a fresh HTTP streaming timeout of its own — a peer that stalls and then answers 429/503 can hold a single batch for attempts x streaming-timeout. This timeout is what keeps that bounded: it is set once, before the first attempt, so every retry shares it instead of each starting its own window.\n\nThe announcement path (individual missing-tx fetches) already shares one streaming window per batch today, http_streaming_timeout, which defaults to 10m (settings.conf). This setting matches that window so the announcement path is unaffected. The block-validation path (CheckBlockSubtrees) previously had no per-batch cap at all, only the much larger 30m gRPC deadline on the whole call; this setting adds the first per-batch cap there.\n\n### Recommendations\n- **10m** (default) - matches the existing http_streaming_timeout window on the announcement path, and adds a first-time per-batch cap on the block path\n- Increase only if legitimate peers are slow to serve transaction batches on demand\n- A non-positive value is treated as the default; the fetch is never left unbounded"`
	PercentageMissingGetFullData              float64             `key:"subtreevalidation_percentageMissingGetFullData" desc:"Percentage threshold for fetching full data" default:"20" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"If more than this % missing, fetch full data" type:"float64" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSets the percentage threshold for switching from individual transaction fetches to full subtree data fetch.\n\n### How It Works\nWhen validating a subtree, if the percentage of missing transactions exceeds this threshold, the system fetches the complete subtree data file instead of individual transactions. This optimizes network usage when most transactions are missing.\n\n### Trade-offs\n| Setting | Benefit | Drawback |\n|---------|---------|----------|\n| Lower | Earlier switch to bulk fetch | May fetch unnecessary data |\n| Higher | More selective fetching | More round-trips when many missing |\n\n### Recommendations\n- **20** (default) - Good balance for typical scenarios\n- Increase for nodes with high cache hit rates\n- Decrease for syncing nodes that are catching up"`
	BlacklistedBaseURLs                       map[string]struct{} `key:"subtreevalidation_blacklisted_baseurls" desc:"Blacklisted base URLs for subtree validation (pipe-separated)" default:"" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"URLs to reject for security" type:"map[string]struct{}" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSpecifies base URLs that should be rejected for subtree fetching.\n\n### How It Works\nWhen peers provide base URLs for fetching subtree data, URLs in this blacklist are rejected. This prevents fetching data from known malicious or unreliable sources.\n\n### Format\nPipe-separated list of URLs (e.g., \"http://bad1.example.com|http://bad2.example.com\")\n\n### Recommendations\n- Add URLs of known malicious peers\n- Add URLs that consistently provide invalid data\n- Leave empty for normal operation"`
	BlockHeightRetentionAdjustment            int32               `key:"subtreevalidation_blockHeightRetentionAdjustment" desc:"Adjustment to global block height retention" default:"0" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Can be positive or negative" type:"int32" longdesc:"### Purpose\nAdjusts the global retention policy specifically for subtree data.\n\n### How It Works\nThe Delete-At-Height (DAH) for subtrees is calculated as: global_retention + this_adjustment. This allows subtree data to be retained longer or pruned sooner than the global setting.\n\n### Values\n- **0** (default) - Use global retention setting\n- **Positive** - Keep subtrees longer than global setting\n- **Negative** - Prune subtrees sooner than global setting\n\n### Recommendations\n- **0** - For most deployments\n- Positive values for nodes serving historical data to syncing peers\n- Negative values only if subtree storage is constrained"`
	CheckBlockSubtreesConcurrency             int                 `key:"subtreevalidation_check_block_subtrees_concurrency" desc:"Concurrency limit for CheckBlockSubtrees operations" default:"32" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Parallel subtree check operations" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nControls the number of parallel workers for checking block subtrees during validation.\n\n### How It Works\nWhen validating a block, subtrees are loaded and processed in parallel. Each worker handles one subtree, fetching data from storage or peers as needed.\n\n### Trade-offs\n| Setting | Benefit | Drawback |\n|---------|---------|----------|\n| Higher | Faster block validation | More CPU, memory, and network usage |\n| Lower | Lower resource usage | Slower block validation |\n\n### Recommendations\n- **32** (default) - Good balance for most multi-core systems\n- Increase for systems with high-IOPS storage\n- Decrease for resource-constrained environments"`
	PauseTimeout                              time.Duration       `key:"subtreevalidation_pauseTimeout" desc:"Maximum duration for subtree processing pauses" default:"5m" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Maximum pause during block validation" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSets the maximum duration that subtree processing can be paused during block validation coordination.\n\n### How It Works\nDuring block validation, subtree processing may be paused to coordinate with other services. This timeout prevents indefinite pauses that would stall validation.\n\n### Recommendations\n- **5m** (default) - Allows for coordination of large blocks\n- Increase for very large blocks or slow systems\n- Decrease to fail faster if coordination should be quick"`
	TxBatchSize                               int                 `key:"subtreevalidation_check_block_subtrees_tx_batch_size" desc:"Transaction batch size for CheckBlockSubtrees" default:"1048576" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"0 = no batching, otherwise batch size" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nControls the number of transactions processed per batch during CheckBlockSubtrees operations.\n\n### How It Works\nWhen validating blocks with many subtrees, transactions are grouped into batches to manage memory usage. The batch size determines how many subtrees are loaded at once based on their transaction count.\n\n### Values\n- **0** - Disable batching, process all subtrees at once\n- **1048576** (default) - Process approximately 1M transactions per batch\n\n### Trade-offs\n| Setting | Benefit | Drawback |\n|---------|---------|----------|\n| Larger | Fewer batch transitions | Higher memory usage |\n| Smaller | Lower memory usage | More batch transitions |\n\n### Recommendations\n- **1048576** (default) - Good balance for most systems\n- Reduce for memory-constrained environments\n- Set to 0 for small blocks to avoid batching overhead"`
	SpendAndCreateMultiMaxTxs                 int                 `key:"subtreevalidation_spendAndCreateMultiMaxTxs" desc:"Most transactions per SpendAndCreateMulti list during catch-up" default:"1048576" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Transactions per store list" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nDuring catch-up above the checkpoint, CheckBlockSubtrees checks a load batch of transactions on all cores and then writes them to the UTXO store with SpendAndCreateMulti, one list at a time, in block order. This caps the transactions in one list.\n\n### Trade-offs\nThe default matches subtreevalidation_check_block_subtrees_tx_batch_size, so a load batch is normally one list. A smaller list splits the block: each list pays its own existence check, and a dependency level cut across two lists pays its spend and create round trips twice, which makes deep blocks slower. A list holds pointers to transactions the load batch already holds, plus one result per transaction, until it returns. No store holds a database transaction open across a list, so no lock depends on the size."`
	UseOrderedLevelAlgorithm                  bool                `key:"subtreevalidation_useOrderedLevelAlgorithm" desc:"Use optimized ordered level algorithm" default:"true" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Assumes transactions are ordered for O(V*I) complexity" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nEnables an optimized algorithm that assumes transactions are topologically ordered within subtrees.\n\n### How It Works\nThe Bitcoin protocol mandates that transactions within a block are ordered such that parent transactions appear before their children. When enabled, the algorithm exploits this ordering to calculate dependency levels in a single pass.\n\n### Values\n- **true** (default) - Use optimized O(V*I) algorithm\n- **false** - Use standard O(V*E + V^2) algorithm\n\nWhere V = transactions, I = average inputs per transaction, E = dependencies.\n\n### Recommendations\n- **true** (default) - For all compliant block sources\n- Set to **false** only when receiving blocks from non-compliant sources with unordered transactions"`
	BlocksOnly                                bool                `key:"subtreevalidation_blocks_only" desc:"Only process subtrees from blocks, skip peer-announced subtrees" default:"false" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"When true, skip processing subtrees from Kafka (peer announcements)" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nWhen enabled, the node only processes subtrees that come from blocks (i.e. during block validation). Peer-announced subtrees received via Kafka are skipped.\n\n### How It Works\n- **false** (default) - Process all subtrees: both peer-announced (Kafka) and block-subtrees (CheckBlockSubtrees)\n- **true** - Skip processing subtrees from Kafka (peer announcements). Only process subtrees when they are referenced in a block being validated.\n\n### Use Cases\n- Lightweight nodes that only need block data\n- Reduced bandwidth and CPU for nodes not participating in mempool/subtree propagation\n- Similar to listen_only but for subtree processing\n\n### Recommendations\n- **false** (default) - Full participation, process all subtrees\n- **true** - For lightweight nodes or when minimizing resource usage"`
	TxPolicyRejectedCacheEnabled              bool                `key:"subtreevalidation_txPolicyRejectedCacheEnabled" desc:"Enable cache for policy-rejected transactions" default:"true" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Cache policy-rejected txs to avoid fetching from peers" type:"bool" longdesc:"### Purpose\nEnables an in-memory cache of transactions that were rejected due to local policy but are consensus-valid.\n\n### How It Works\n- Populated from the KAFKA_TX_POLICY_REJECTED topic\n- When validating a subtree, missing transactions are looked up in this cache before requesting from the originating miner\n- Transactions from this cache are NOT blessed — they still require full validation via blessMissingTransaction\n\n### Values\n- **true** (default) - Enable caching for faster subtree validation when other miners include policy-violating txs\n- **false** - Always fetch from peers\n\n### Recommendations\n- Keep enabled for all production deployments"`
	TxPolicyRejectedCacheMaxMB                int                 `key:"subtreevalidation_txPolicyRejectedCacheMaxMB" desc:"Maximum memory in MB for each of the policy-rejected and kept-aside transaction caches" default:"64" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Increase for nodes seeing many policy-rejected txs" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSets the maximum memory in megabytes for each of two caches: the policy-rejected transaction cache and the kept-aside transaction cache. Their combined memory use is up to twice this value.\n\n### How It Works\nThe policy-rejected cache stores raw transaction bytes for transactions the validator rejected on local policy or as conflicting. The kept-aside cache stores transactions subtree validation kept aside because they spend a conflicting transaction; it has its own budget so a flood of them cannot evict the validator's entries. A larger cache retains more transactions, increasing the chance of cache hits during subtree validation.\n\n### Recommendations\n- **64** (default) - Suitable for most deployments\n- Increase if other miners frequently include policy-violating transactions"`
	KeptAsideMaxBytesPerSubtree               int                 `key:"subtreevalidation_keptAsideMaxBytesPerSubtree" desc:"Maximum bytes of conflicting txs kept aside from one peer subtree" default:"16777216" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Raise only if honest subtrees with many double spends fail" type:"int" longdesc:"### Purpose\nBounds the conflicting transactions, and their descendants, that subtree validation keeps aside from a single peer subtree until a block references it.\n\n### How It Works\nThe kept-aside transactions the peer sent are written next to the subtree. When they exceed this many bytes, the subtree fails validation as it did before transactions were kept aside, and the block that references it fetches and validates it again.\n\n### Recommendations\n- **16777216** (16 MB, default) - far above the double spends an honest subtree carries\n- Lower to limit the disk a peer can fill with kept-aside transactions"`
	CheckBlockSubtreesTimeout                 time.Duration       `key:"subtreevalidation_check_block_subtrees_timeout" desc:"Timeout for CheckBlockSubtrees gRPC calls" default:"30m" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Maximum time for block subtree validation" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSets the maximum time for a CheckBlockSubtrees gRPC call to complete before timing out.\n\n### How It Works\nWhen block validation calls the subtree validation service to validate all subtrees in a block, this timeout ensures the call does not hang indefinitely due to stale gRPC connections or unresponsive services.\n\n### Trade-offs\n| Setting | Benefit | Drawback |\n|---------|---------|----------|\n| Shorter | Faster recovery from stale connections | May timeout on very large blocks |\n| Longer | Tolerates slow validation | Slower detection of stuck connections |\n\n### Recommendations\n- **30m** (default) - Generous for large blocks during catchup\n- Reduce for environments where faster failure recovery is preferred"`
	TxMetaTombstoneWindow                     time.Duration       `key:"subtreevalidation_txMetaTombstoneWindow" desc:"How long a txmeta DELETE keeps the demoted tx out of the txmeta cache" default:"30m" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"Must exceed the worst consumer lag on the txmeta topic" type:"duration" longdesc:"### Purpose\nSets how long subtree validation remembers a txmeta DELETE (a tx demoted to conflicting) so that a late ADD for the same tx is not cached.\n\n### How It Works\nThe validator's ADD and block assembly's DELETE for one tx travel on different partitions of the txmeta topic, so the DELETE can be consumed first. While remembered, the txmeta cache evicts any write of the tx right after it lands: a late ADD, or a read-through write whose store read raced the demotion. Without it the tx is cached as non-conflicting again. Entries are kept for between one and two windows. Memory is roughly 60 MB per million demoted txs.\n\n### Recommendations\n- **30m** (default) - Far above normal consumer lag\n- Must exceed the worst lag a txmeta consumer can fall behind, which can be longer than the topic's retention because Kafka deletes whole segments\n- Must be positive; the service refuses to start otherwise"`
	MaxIncomingSubtreeBytes                   int64               `key:"subtreevalidation_max_incoming_subtree_bytes" desc:"Maximum bytes accepted when fetching a peer's subtree node body" default:"134217728" category:"SubtreeValidation" usage:"DoS cap for incoming peer subtree responses" type:"int64" longdesc:"### Purpose\nCaps the response body size accepted when fetching subtree node data from a peer.\n\n### How It Works\nApplied via io.LimitReader on responses from the peer's /subtree/<hash> endpoint. A peer that streams more than this is treated as malicious (or misconfigured) and the fetch is aborted with ErrExternal.\n\n### Why a Receive-Side Policy\nThe limit must be independent of the local BlockAssembly.MaximumMerkleItemsPerSubtree, which controls what *this node assembles*, not what peers send. Bounding incoming subtrees by local assembly policy breaks catchup whenever the local cap is smaller than the network's actual subtree size (e.g. docker quickstart syncing teratestnet).\n\n### Values\n- **134217728** (128 MiB, default) - Generous headroom over the default mainnet subtree size (1M items x 32 bytes = 32 MiB)\n- Increase if the network norm grows; decrease only if you know your peers' subtree sizes\n\n### Recommendations\nLeave at the default unless you have measured the largest legitimate subtree on your network. Lowering this is the wrong knob to tune for local memory pressure - tune BlockAssembly settings instead."`
}
